NAME
       lsblk - list block devices (disks, partitions, logical volumes) as a tree

SYNOPSIS
       lsblk [-f] [-p] [-n] [-l] [-d] [-b] [-o COLUMNS] [DEVICE...]

DESCRIPTION
       lsblk shows every disk with what is on it: partitions under their disk,
       logical volumes under the partition (physical volume) they live on,
       and where each is mounted or whether it is swap:

           NAME          MAJ:MIN RM  SIZE RO TYPE MOUNTPOINTS
           vda           252:0    0   20G  0 disk
           |-vda1        252:1    0    1G  0 part /boot
           `-vda2        252:2    0   19G  0 part
             |-rhel-root 253:0    0   17G  0 lvm  /
             `-rhel-swap 253:1    0    2G  0 lvm  [SWAP]
           vdb           252:16   0    5G  0 disk
           sr0            11:0    1  9.8G  0 rom

       It is the quickest way to see which disks are free, how big they are,
       and whether a new partition or LV appeared. Unlike df it also shows
       things that are not mounted. TYPE is disk, part, lvm, rom, loop...

OPTIONS
       -f, --fs
              Show the file systems instead: FSTYPE, FSVER, LABEL, UUID,
              FSAVAIL, FSUSE% and MOUNTPOINTS.

       -p, --paths
              Full device paths (/dev/vdb1) instead of names.

       -o LIST, --output LIST
              Choose the columns, separated by commas: NAME, PATH, SIZE,
              TYPE, FSTYPE, LABEL, UUID, PARTUUID, MOUNTPOINT, MOUNTPOINTS,
              RO, RM, MODEL, SERIAL, OWNER, GROUP, MODE, PKNAME, KNAME,
              MAJ:MIN, FSSIZE, FSUSED, FSAVAIL, FSUSE%.

       -n, --noheadings
              No header line.

       -l, --list
              A flat list instead of a tree.

       -r, --raw
              Raw output, for scripts.

       -d, --nodeps
              Only the disks, without their partitions.

       -b, --bytes
              Sizes in bytes.

       -a, --all
              Include empty devices.

       -m, --perms
              Show owner, group and mode of the device files.

       -S, --scsi
              Only SCSI devices.

       -J, --json
              JSON output.

EXAMPLES
       What disks are there, and what is on them?

           lsblk

       File system types, labels and UUIDs:

           lsblk -f

       Only one disk, after partitioning it:

           lsblk /dev/vdb

       The size of a partition in bytes, for a script:

           lsblk -b -n -o SIZE /dev/vdb1

SEE ALSO
       blkid(8), findmnt(8), df(1), fdisk(8), lvs(8)
